Key Points & Considerations

    Out-of-core reading:
        Using vaex.from_csv(..., convert=True) creates an HDF5/Arrow file for faster subsequent loads. This is particularly useful for very large CSVs.
        Once the data is in .hdf5 or .arrow format, Vaex can map it without loading the entire dataset into RAM.

    In-memory steps:
        SMOTE, GridSearchCV, and SHAP require in-memory arrays (NumPy/pandas). Thus, after loading with Vaex, we call .to_pandas_df() to convert. For truly massive datasets, you would need alternative approaches (e.g., partial fitting, or chunk-based computations).

    Preprocessing:
        Vaex handles missing values, infinity replacements, etc., via expressions. You can do more sophisticated transformations (e.g., filtering, outlier clipping) directly on Vaex DataFrames before converting to NumPy.

    Scalability:
        If the dataset is larger than available memory, be mindful when calling .to_pandas_df(). You might need chunk-based approaches or sampling.

In short, Vaex offers an out-of-core strategy for reading and manipulating large files, but many scikit-learn operations (including cross-validation, SMOTE, and custom model training) still require in-memory arrays. This script outlines a hybrid pipeline that uses Vaex primarily for I/O and preliminary cleansing, then hands off to NumPy-based workflows.

    Reading the Same Data
    We call vaex.from_csv(...) for each file (e.g., S1_extended_encoded_X_train.csv) exactly like your pandas-based version. If your files are large, consider convert=True for faster subsequent loads (Vaex will create an Arrow/HDF5 cache).

    Combining X and y
    In the pandas script, you simply have two DataFrames. With Vaex, you can’t just do X['target'] = y['col'] unless you’re sure rows match perfectly. Here we explicitly copy out the NumPy array from the y DataFrame and assign it to X_vaex. The function combine_features_and_target does exactly this, creating a single DataFrame with both features and the label.

    Preprocessing
        We replace NaNs, ±∞ with 0 in each numeric column in both train and test sets, all out-of-core.
        Scaling (like StandardScaler) is not shown here. If you want to replicate that, you could compute each column’s mean/std in the training set (via train_df[col].mean(), train_df[col].std()) and then create a virtual column df[f"{col}_scaled"] = (df[col] - mean) / std. Then those scaled columns become your features.

    Fitting
    VaexGaussianNB.fit uses df.groupby(...) on the label column to compute count, mean, and var for each feature. This is done out-of-core on the entire training set. The resulting groupby is used to populate the class priors, means, and variances.

    Chunked Prediction & Metrics
        evaluate_chunked calls df.to_items(...) with a chunk_size to read a subset of rows at a time.
        Each chunk is converted to a NumPy array for the model’s predict_chunk.
        We accumulate predictions in a confusion matrix so we never hold the entire predicted set in memory.
        Finally, we compute micro-averaged precision, recall, F1, and accuracy for the entire dataset.

    Results
        We store each dataset’s final metrics in aggregate_metrics.
        At the end, we convert it to a pandas DataFrame for display/saving as CSV.

Adapting to Large Data & Additional Features

    Partial Fitting: If even grouping the entire training data at once is too large, you could manually do incremental mean/variance updates per chunk. However, Vaex’s out-of-core aggregations usually suffice for many GB-level datasets (depending on your memory).
    SMOTE, Cross-Validation: These rely on in-memory arrays, so you’d either:
        Convert Vaex DataFrames to NumPy on a smaller sample.
        Use alternative out-of-core or approximate methods.
        Implement custom chunk-based oversampling. (Advanced)
    SHAP: Tools like SHAP also expect in-memory data. For extremely large data, you might sample a fraction of your dataset for SHAP interpretations.

With this setup, you can load exactly the same CSVs used in your pandas pipeline, keep the data out-of-core in Vaex, and still compute total (global) metrics on the test set by chunking.